    "breadcrumb": "",
    "content": "Course Description\rThis course provides an introduction to fixed income markets and the analytical tools used by market participants. Fixed income markets have evolved significantly in recent years, particularly following the global financial crisis. Regulatory changes, unconventional monetary policy and extended periods of very low or near-zero nominal interest rates, increased government borrowing, shifts in the empirical behavior of U.S. Treasury securities, and the introduction of new fixed income instruments have all influenced how these markets function and how fixed income securities are priced and risk-managed.\nFixed income is a broad topic that cannot be covered comprehensively in a single semester. Accordingly, the course is selective and focuses on the core concepts and methods that underpin fixed income valuation and risk management. With a strong foundation in the underlying theory and market conventions, students will be well-positioned to learn additional institutional details and specialized products in professional settings.\nThe course is quantitative and emphasizes practical implementation. Students will work extensively with formulas, numerical methods, and real-world valuation and hedging techniques widely used in industry, including by banks and asset managers.\nLearning Objectives\rAfter completing this course, students will be able to:\nDescribe the structure of bond markets, the main fixed income instruments, and the conventions used to quote, trade, and price fixed income securities. Explain the U.S. Treasury market, including major Treasury instruments and the mechanisms through which Treasuries are issued and traded. Apply time-value-of-money principles to price fixed income cash flows and interpret discount factors and their role in valuation. Use replicating portfolios and the law of one price to value fixed income securities and identify arbitrage relationships. Analyze the term structure of interest rates and use it to price bonds and other fixed income instruments. Explain the bond price-yield relationship and quantify interest rate sensitivity using standard measures. Compute and apply duration and convexity to measure interest rate risk and to hedge single-bond positions and bond portfolios. Implement and evaluate common fixed income trading strategies. Understand the mechanics and uses of forward and futures contracts in fixed income markets. Price and interpret key interest rate derivatives, especially interest rate swaps, and apply them to hedge interest rate risk. Class Times\rFINC 409/609 Day(s) Tue, Thu Time Section 010: 9.35am-10.55am Section 011: 11.10am-12.30pm Location Gore Hall 219 Office Hours\rSection FINC 409/609 Day(s) Tuesdays Time 4.00pm-5.30pm Location 310 Purnell Hall Exam Schedule\rMidterm 1 Date: March 12, 2026 Time: 9.35am-10.55am (Section 010); 11.10am-12.30pm (Section 011) Location: Gore Hall Hall 219 Midterm 2 Date: April 14, 2026 Time: 9.35am-10.55am (Section 010); 11.10am-12.30pm (Section 011) Location: Gore Hall Hall 219 Final Exam Section 010: Date: May 19, 2026 Time: 8.00am-10.00am Location: Gore Hall 219 Section 011: Date: May 16, 2026 Time: 11.30am-1.30pm Location: Gore Hall 219 Course Syllabus\r📄 Show Syllabus",
